
        <worksheet xml:id="sec-mid2-rev">
        <title>Midterm 2 Concept Check: Multi-variable Calculus and Vector Spaces</title>
        <exercise workspace="10in">
            <title>Midterm Review</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The following conceptual questions are intended to help you review important ideas covered on midterm 2 (<q>Partial Derivatives</q> through <q>Bases</q>).
                    You should use them, along with homework questions, lab questions, and class examples, to guide your studying and creation
                    of a single note sheet that can be used during the in-class midterm. Midterm questions will be written so 
                    that a calculator is not needed.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                               How do you compute partial derivatives? In 3D, what does the value of a partial derivative at a 
                               point tell you about the behavior of the surface at that point?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                How do you compute the tangent plane to a 3D surface at a point? Why are tangent planes useful?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            What is a directional derivative? How are directional derivatives related to partial derivatives?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            What is the gradient vector at a point, and what information does it hold?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            What is the total differential of a multi-variable function at a point? What is it used for?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            How do you represent the multi-variable chain rule using tree diagrams?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            What does a double integral represent geometrically?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            What do the bounds of a double integral look like when integrating over a rectangular region? How
                            does this change if your region of integration is non-rectangular?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            When is it possible to visualize a triple integral geometrically? What types of applications are 
                            triple integrals commonly used for?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            We've discussed transforming from rectangular coordinates to
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        polar coordinates
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        cylindrical coordinates
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        spherical coordinates
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                            What is the Jacobian of each of these three transformations? When do you need to use the Jacobian
                            for each of these three transformations, and why?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            Why are multi-variable integrals in non-rectangular coordinates common in quantum mechanics (review Homework 5 carefully).
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            In <m>\R^2</m>, what is the meaning of a <q>vector</q>? How do we visualize scalar multiplication of vectors and addition of vectors? What is a unit vector?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            What notions do we have of multiplying vectors? What does each concept mean geometrically?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            What is a vector space? What are examples of vector spaces, and how are they similar/dissimilar 
                            to the most familiar vector space we've seen, <m>\R^2</m>?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            What is a Hilbert space? Why might we want to add an inner product to a vector space?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            What is the standard inner product used for complex function spaces (like the function 
                            space of wave functions in quantum mechanics)?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            What is a basis for a vector space?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            What are examples of bases for vector spaces that we've seen? How do we use a basis to define
                            the dimension of a vector space?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                       <li>
                        <p>
                            Can changing the set of scalars for a vector space change its dimension? Why?
                        </p>
                       </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>

            </statement>
            
        </exercise>
      

        </worksheet>
